The Responses API returns web citations as **annotations on output text**, not as a separate top-level citations array. Enable search with `tools: [{ type: "web_search" }]` ([official quickstart](https://platform.openai.com/docs/quickstart/make-your-first-api-request)).

- `response.output` contains output items, including the assistant `message` and possibly a `web_search_call`.
- A message’s `content` contains an `output_text` part.
- `content.annotations` contains citation objects with `type: "url_citation"`, `url`, `title`, `start_index`, and `end_index`. The indexes identify the cited span in the text ([API reference](https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/responses-streaming/response/output_text/annotation/added?lang=node.js)).
- `response.output_text` is a convenient plain-text aggregate; inspect `output` to obtain citation metadata.

```js
import OpenAI from "openai";

const client = new OpenAI();

const response = await client.responses.create({
  model: "gpt-5",
  tools: [{ type: "web_search" }],
  input: "What was a positive news story from today?",
});

console.log(response.output_text);

for (const item of response.output ?? []) {
  for (const part of item.content ?? []) {
    for (const citation of part.annotations ?? []) {
      if (citation.type === "url_citation") {
        console.log(citation.title, citation.url);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

browser.answer:
The Responses API returns citations inside `response.output`, not as a separate top-level citations array:

- `web_search_call`: records the search (`id`, `status`, and `action` such as `search`).
- `message.content[]`: contains the assistant’s answer.
- An `output_text` content item contains `text` plus `annotations[]`.
- Each web citation is a `url_citation` with `url`, `title`, `start_index`, and `end_index`, identifying the cited source and its position in the text. Render these URLs as clickable links. ([OpenAI web-search guide](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/tools-web-search), [API reference](https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/typescript/resources/responses/))

```js
import OpenAI from "openai";
const openai = new OpenAI();

const r = await openai.responses.create({
  model: "gpt-5.6",
  tools: [{ type: "web_search" }],
  input: "What is the tallest mountain in the world?"
});

for (const item of r.output) {
  if (item.type !== "message") continue;
  for (const part of item.content) {
    if (part.type !== "output_text") continue;

    console.log(part.text);
    for (const c of part.annotations ?? []) {
      if (c.type === "url_citation")
        console.log(c.title, c.url, c.start_index, c.end_index);
    }
  }
}
```

To retrieve every consulted URL—not only URLs cited inline—add `include: ["web_search_call.action.sources"]`.

`Promise.try(callback, ...args)` synchronously invokes the callback and wraps its outcome in a promise:

- synchronous return → fulfilled promise;
- synchronous throw → rejected promise;
- returned promise → promise adopts its eventual fulfillment/rejection.

Unlike `Promise.resolve(callback())`, it catches errors thrown while invoking the callback. With `Promise.resolve(callback())`, JavaScript evaluates `callback()` first, so a synchronous exception escapes immediately rather than becoming a rejection. If the callback does not throw, both generally adopt its returned value or promise. [`Promise.try()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/try) also accepts callback arguments directly.

**Availability on July 20, 2026:** MDN classifies it as **Baseline: Newly available**—supported across the latest devices and browser versions since **January 2025**. Older browsers may not support it, so feature detection or a polyfill may be needed for legacy targets. [`Promise.resolve()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/resolve) confirms that evaluating its argument can synchronously throw without being wrapped.

In a **STRICT** table, `ANY` accepts values of any storage class—integers, real numbers, text, or BLOBs—and preserves both the value and its datatype exactly as inserted. No type coercion occurs (subject to constraints such as `NOT NULL`). ([SQLite: STRICT Tables](https://www.sqlite.org/stricttables.html#the_any_datatype))

In an ordinary **non-STRICT** table, `ANY` is still flexible, but normal SQLite affinity rules apply: strings that look like numbers are converted to numeric storage when possible. Thus:

```sql
-- STRICT
CREATE TABLE t(a ANY) STRICT;
INSERT INTO t VALUES('000123');
-- typeof(a): text; value: '000123'

-- Non-STRICT
CREATE TABLE t(a ANY);
INSERT INTO t VALUES('000123');
-- typeof(a): integer; value: 123
```

So, the key difference is that STRICT `ANY` is an exact-preservation type, whereas non-STRICT `ANY` may coerce numeric-looking text.
